1.0-1
   o	The redirect of a static directory request without the
	trailing slash percent-encodes the path in the Location:
	header. Previously, paths with spaces, % or other special
	characters resulted in an invalid location.


1.0-0	2026-11-12
   o	webstart() accepts connections on one or more listeners
	specified by listen=, each a port number (local HTTP),
	"http://<host>:<port>", "https://<host>:<port>" or "unix:<path>"
	(local socket, unix only). <host> can be "localhost" (local
	connections only), "*" (all IPv4 interfaces), a numeric IPv4
	address or an IPv6 address in [] (e.g., "[::1]" or "[::]").
	IPv6 listeners only accept IPv6 connections on all systems, so
	"https://*" and "https://[::]" together serve both. simple()
	passes further arguments (such as threads) on to webstart().

   o	TLS/SSL (HTTPS) support. Listeners using "https://" require a
	server certificate and its private key (PEM format) specified
	with tls.cert and tls.key. The key defaults to tls.cert, so a
	single file with both is sufficient. The minimal configuration
	is

	  webstart("https://*:8443", tls.cert="server.crt",
	           tls.key="server.key")

	If the certificate was issued by an intermediate CA, its
	certificate(s) must be sent along with the server certificate:
	either append them to the tls.cert file (after the server
	certificate) or specify a separate file with tls.ca.

	HTTP and HTTPS listeners can be combined, e.g., to serve local
	clients without encryption:

	  webstart(c(8080, "https://*:8443"), tls.cert="server.pem")

	The tls.* arguments apply to all HTTPS listeners, but can also
	have one value per HTTPS listener, e.g., to use different
	certificates on different ports.

	Clients can be required to authenticate with a certificate:
	tls.client.ca specifies the CA certificate(s) the client
	certificates must be issued by, connections without a valid
	certificate are rejected. tls.client.cn further restricts the
	allowed common names of the client certificates, a name can
	start or end with * to match a suffix or a prefix:

	  webstart("https://*:8443", tls.cert="server.pem",
	           tls.client.ca="corp-ca.pem",
	           tls.client.cn=c("alice", "*.corp.example.com"))

	The client CA should be dedicated to issuing client certificates
	(e.g., the organisation's own CA), since every certificate it
	issued is accepted unless tls.client.cn is used.

	TLS 1.2 or higher is used, encrypted private keys are not
	supported. TLS requires OpenSSL 1.1.0 or higher at build time,
	hasTLS() reports whether TLS is supported.

   o	R handlers receive the headers Request-Proto (http or https,
	depending on the listener) and, if the client presented a valid
	certificate, Request-Client-CN with its common name, so handlers
	can identify clients. Header names starting with Request- are
	reserved for the server, such headers sent by the client
	(including the already present Request-Method) are dropped.

   o	R handlers can decode the request body: addHandler("rfun", ...)
	has a parse.body argument which is either FALSE (default, raw
	body), TRUE (use defaultBodyParsers()) or a named list of
	parsers function(body, content.type) where the names are media
	types, optionally starting or ending with * to match a suffix
	or prefix (e.g., "*+json" or "text/*"). The default
	parsers decode URL encoded forms into a named character vector
	(like the query) and JSON (using jsonlite). If the
	body cannot be parsed, the request is answered with 400 Bad
	Request without calling the handler, e.g.,

	  addHandler("rfun", "/api", FUN, parse.body=TRUE)

	simple() uses parse.body=TRUE by default, so its R handler
	receives decoded form and JSON bodies (parse.body=FALSE passes
	the raw body).

   o	folded header lines (obsolete since HTTP/1.1) are rejected with
	400 Bad Request.

   o	TLS support was never enabled on Windows (wrong macro in the
	Windows configuration) and configure enabled it even when no
	OpenSSL was found.

   o	if a listener cannot be started, the error now includes the
	reason (e.g., address already in use).

   o	an existing file at the path of a unix socket is no longer
	removed unless it is a socket.

   o	forked children (fork=TRUE) no longer try to re-seed the
	system RNG (the code was never active). They inherit the R RNG
	state of the parent, see ?webstart for how to get independent
	random streams.

   o	forked children now exit with _exit() and stdio is flushed
	before fork(). Previously a child could emit output buffered
	by the parent again or, when run by Rscript, make the parent
	continue reading its script from the wrong position.

   o	use withVisible() instead of the non-API R_Visible in
	R >= 4.6.0.

   o	support chunked request bodies (Transfer-Encoding: chunked).
	The chunks are collected into the request body, so handlers
	see the same as with Content-Length. Other transfer codings
	are still answered with 501, Transfer-Encoding together with
	Content-Length, in HTTP/1.0 or with GET/HEAD with 400.

   o	reject header fields with whitespace between the name and the
	colon with 400 (RFC 9112, 5.1). Previously such Content-Length
	or Transfer-Encoding fields were ignored, so the body was parsed
	as the next request (request smuggling).

   o	1xx, 204 and 304 responses no longer include a body or
	Content-Length, which broke the framing of keep-alive
	connections.

   o	reject requests with more than one Host: header with 400
	(RFC 9112, 3.2).

   o	Connection: is parsed as a list of tokens, so e.g.
	"Connection: keep-alive, close" closes the connection.

   o	HTTP/1.1 responses include Connection: close if the server
	will close the connection (RFC 9112, 9.6).

   o	fix a crash when the header buffer cannot be allocated.

   o	R handlers can close the connection by including
	Connection: close in their headers.

   o	R handlers evaluated on processing threads (threads > 0) now
	have working C stack checks using the stack of the thread.
	Previously the checks were disabled, so runaway recursion
	could crash the server instead of raising an R error.

   o	fix compilation with the UCRT headers of Rtools 4.x
	(redefinition of timespec_get()).

   o	Windows: fix a possible crash when creating the internal
	socket pair fails (bad format string in the error message).

   o	fix compilation on Linux with gcc 15 (implicit declaration
	of pthread_setname_np()).

   o	Windows: fix a possible crash with threads > 0 where the
	server loop checked for user interrupts while a processing
	thread was evaluating R.
